title: Reverting everything since a time
tags: [[The operation log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: A rollback is a choice of set: the user's operations written after the time and still standing — changes, and reverts of older operations — reverted onto the current state.

With the log a sequence ([[The operation log]]), going "back to this morning" is not a
navigation but one more revert ([[Reverting onto the current state]]): the target
`{"since": T}` (Unix ms; `target_since=` on the plan, [[Revert endpoints]]). Asked of
the user in [[Rollback]]. Proposed with the log's redesign, deferred by the user on 12
October 2026, built on 8 November 2026.

!! The cut

The daemon finds the newest operation written at or before T (`last_op_at_or_before`, a
binary search over the ids); everything after it is "since". No operation at or before T
is a `404`: the state then is not known. Retention trims each origin apart ([[Automatic retention]]), so the check is on the whole log, not the user's operations alone: a user
history trimmed past T while the watcher's reaches further back is not detected — with
the default budgets (a million user operations, 50 000 of the watcher's) it does not happen.

!! The set

Of the operations after the cut, `revert::since` keeps those that:

* are not the watcher's (`origin = watcher`), as undo does ([[The undo selection]]) — a trashing and a reconcile are the user's;
* still stand — no standing revert of them, resolved along the chain;
* are either an ordinary change, or a revert of an operation ''at or before the cut'' — an undo of older work, or a redo of an older undo. Reverting it brings that work back, or undoes it again, as it stood at T.

A revert of an operation written ''after'' the cut is left out: either it still stands and
its target does not (the two cancel, nothing of either was there at T), or it was itself
reverted and does not stand. Hence "changes" alone (`only: changes`) would not do: an undo
since T of an older change would stay, and the state would not be T's.

This was the choice put to the user (8 November 2026): "the state at T", over "your changes
since T" only.

!! What it writes

One revert, in the request's context or a label of its own named after the time (`rollback to
2026-11-08T09:00:00Z`); undoing that label takes the rollback back like any other. `unknown` operations in
the set fail it, as any revert of one does.

!! Cost

The cut costs a few keys; the set reads every operation since — the watcher's included,
then dropped — and the reverters of each kept one. A rollback over a large reconcile reads
it whole. It is an explicit, rare command; nothing bounds it.
